\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Ambiguity in NLP and requirements engineering.}
Ambiguity has been studied extensively in both computational linguistics and software engineering. Word sense disambiguation \citep{navigli_wsd_2009} addresses lexical ambiguity at the token level; our lexical type extends this to policy-specific polysemy. \citet{li_ambiguity_taxonomy_2024} propose an 11-type NLP taxonomy covering syntactic, semantic, and pragmatic ambiguity, from which we draw our linguistic-layer types. \citet{kamath_scope_2024} investigate scopal ambiguity in LLMs, finding systematic preferences for surface scope. In requirements engineering (RE), \citet{massey_ambiguity_re_2014} classify six ambiguity types in regulatory texts, and \citet{berry_kamsties_2004} introduce the distinction between nocuous ambiguity (where stakeholders diverge on interpretation) and innocuous ambiguity (where they converge). Computational detection of nocuous ambiguity has progressed from heuristic methods \citep{chantree_2006,willis_nocuous_2008} through NLP-based cross-domain detectors \citep{ferrari_ambiguity_2019,ferrari_defects_2018} to transformer-based approaches for anaphoric ambiguity \citep{ezzini_taphsir_2022,ezzini_anaphoric_2022}. \citet{zhao_nlp4re_2021} survey the broader NLP4RE landscape. Our work extends Berry and Kamsties' framework to LLM agents, where the absence of shared organizational context makes previously innocuous ambiguity nocuous. Our taxonomy serves as an experimental instrument for controlled causal analysis rather than a proposed classification standard.

\paragraph{Psycholinguistics of ambiguity processing.}
\citet{ferreira_good_enough_2002} show that human comprehenders build shallow, ``good-enough'' representations rather than full parses, paralleling our observation that LLM agents adopt surface interpretations. \citet{frazier_rayner_1982} demonstrate early commitment to a single parse; \citet{li_garden_path_llm_2024} provide direct evidence that LLMs exhibit analogous garden-path effects. At the pragmatic level, \citet{hu_pragmatic_2023} and \citet{ruis_goldilocks_2023} show that LLMs struggle with implicature resolution, defaulting to literal interpretations even when pragmatic inference is required. \citet{liu_ambiguity_2023} demonstrate that language models fail to represent ambiguity, collapsing multiple interpretations into a single output; \citet{gur_arieh_ambiguity_collapse_2026} taxonomize the epistemic risks of this ``ambiguity collapse''; \citet{sedova_to_know_2024} show analogous self-inconsistencies under entity-type ambiguity. These findings ground our \emph{disambiguation neglect} result: agents process ambiguous input without meta-cognitive awareness but, unlike humans, lack social channels to recover. \citet{disambiguation_cqa_survey_2025} survey disambiguation strategies in conversational QA; \citet{suri_structured_2025} show that structured uncertainty estimation improves coverage by 7--39\%, while \citet{sehwag_aspi_2026} demonstrate that clarification-seeking introduces prompt injection vulnerabilities---a tension for disambiguation channel design. \citet{vijayvargiya_ambigswe_2026} show that interactive clarification recovers up to 74\% of performance lost to underspecification in SE tasks; our results show agents rarely attempt clarification unprompted.

\paragraph{Agent safety evaluation.}
The agent safety evaluation landscape includes benchmarks for risk awareness \citep{rjudge_2024}, tool-use safety \citep{toolemu_2024}, indirect prompt injection \citep{injecagent_2024}, safe task planning \citep{yin_safeagentbench_2025}, policy-invisible violations \citep{phantompolicy_2026}, comprehensive real-world agent safety \citep{vijayvargiya_openagentsafety_2026}, trajectory-level diagnosis \citep{atbench_2026}, and deployment-condition probing \citep{riskybench_2026}. \citet{mu_can_llms_2023} evaluate rule-following under policy constraints, \citet{tamkin_evaluating_2023} examine how language models navigate policy-governed decisions, \citet{shieldagent_2025} extract formal safety rules from policy documents, and AgentAuditor \citep{agentauditor_2025} achieves human-level safety evaluation, and \citet{weng2026beyond} propose policy invariance as a reliability test for LLM safety judges. Complementary prompting approaches target the agent's decision process directly \citep{hahm_causal_2025}. Our work differs by focusing on the \emph{source specification} rather than the agent's execution: we measure how ambiguity in the policy itself causes safety violations, complementing work that assumes clean policies.

\paragraph{Policy specification for agents.}
DRIP-R \citep{drip_r_2026} is the closest concurrent work: it benchmarks 400 policy-ambiguous retail scenarios across five RE ambiguity types, measuring decision quality rather than safety violations. Our work differs in three respects: (1) our matched-pair design establishes causality rather than correlating ambiguity presence with outcomes; (2) we measure safety violations rather than decision quality, enabling failure mechanism analysis; (3) our two-layer taxonomy adds linguistic-layer types, and we quantify the model$\times$type interaction. Concretely, DRIP-R's correlational design leaves open whether ambiguity \emph{causes} violations or merely co-occurs with harder scenarios; our matched-pair controls ($\Delta_{\text{perturb}} = -0.67$\,pp) isolate the causal contribution. We map DRIP-R's five RE ambiguity categories to our six-type taxonomy in Appendix~\ref{app:drip_mapping}. PolicyBank \citep{policybank_2026} studies iterative policy refinement, closing 82\% of the gap to a human oracle. AgentPex \citep{agentpex_2026} detects procedural violations in agentic traces, finding that 83\% of perfect-outcome traces contain violations. MANTRA \citep{mantra_2026} synthesizes SMT-validated compliance benchmarks from procedural manuals. $\tau$-bench \citep{tau_bench_2024} and its successor $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025} provide the conversational agent evaluation platforms. \citet{yu_causal_2026} demonstrate causal safety degradation from tool affordance, a complementary risk vector; \citet{krakovna2020specification} document specification gaming in RL agents, a reward-specification analogue of our policy-specification findings.

\enlargethispage{10\baselineskip}
\section{Conclusion}

Policy ambiguity increases agent violations by 31.0\,pp in confound-free types ($+$12.7\,pp on SOPBench). The type--mechanism dissociation ($V = 0.45$ vs.\ $0.14$) and $>$89\% disambiguation neglect motivate clause-level policy auditing for similar customer-service settings.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Experimental Platform}

We conduct all experiments on the original release of $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025}, a dual-control conversational agent benchmark spanning retail and airline customer service domains. $\tau^2$-bench provides natural-language policy documents, tool APIs, and multi-turn user interactions with ground-truth evaluation via assertion checking. Its policy injection mechanism allows us to replace individual policy clauses while preserving the surrounding context, enabling controlled ambiguity manipulation.

\subsection{Matched-Pair Design}

For each of the six ambiguity types, we construct 50 \emph{clause pairs}: an ambiguous variant and a matched unambiguous variant that conveys the same intended policy but resolves the ambiguity. Pairs are minimal edits: the unambiguous variant adds only the information necessary to eliminate the target ambiguity (e.g., for scopal ambiguity, replacing ``items may be returned within 30 days'' with ``standard items may be returned within 30 days; customized items are final sale''). This yields 300 clause pairs $\times$ 2 conditions $\times$ 5 models $= 2{,}997$ episodes (3 episodes lost to API errors in one Qwen3 batch).

Clause pairs are generated through a template-based pipeline and reviewed by two independent annotators (both with NLP background) to ensure (1) the ambiguous variant contains exactly one instance of the target type, (2) the unambiguous variant resolves it without changing policy scope, and (3) the ambiguity is plausibly natural. Annotators independently verified type assignments with a 96\% initial agreement (287/300; Cohen's $\kappa = 0.95$ on six-type classification); the 13 disagreements were resolved by discussion, yielding 100\% final consensus. A post-hoc realism validation confirms linguistic consistency with original $\tau^2$-bench policy text (\S\ref{sec:limitations}).

\subsection{Models}

We evaluate five models from four providers spanning two architecture families: GPT-5.4 and GPT-4.1 (OpenAI, proprietary dense), Claude Sonnet 4.6 (Anthropic, proprietary dense), Qwen3-235B (Alibaba, open-source Mixture-of-Experts), and DeepSeek-V3 (DeepSeek, open-source dense). This selection covers frontier and mid-tier proprietary models alongside open-source alternatives with different training philosophies. All API calls use temperature $= 0$, top\_p $= 1$, and max\_tokens $= 4096$, and were made on May 20, 2026.

\subsection{Evaluation}

We use a cross-judge design to mitigate self-evaluation bias: GPT-5.4 judges Claude, GPT-4.1, and Qwen3 traces; GPT-4.1 judges GPT-5.4 and DeepSeek traces. Because our matched-pair design compares ambiguous and unambiguous conditions within the same model--judge pairing, any systematic judge bias cancels in the treatment effect ($\Delta$pp); the cross-judge asymmetry affects only absolute violation rates, not C1 or C2 (\S\ref{sec:limitations}). Judges classify each episode outcome on a four-tier severity scale: none, minor, moderate, and critical. Our primary analysis uses moderate-or-above severity as the violation threshold; minor-only deviations (6.6\% of episodes) are excluded. Including minor violations (overall rate 49.6\% vs.\ 41.0\%) produces even larger effects without changing any directional conclusion.

\paragraph{Cross-judge calibration.} A dual-judge calibration on 100 stratified episodes yields substantial binary agreement ($\kappa = 0.653$; weighted severity $\kappa_w = 0.775$; McNemar $p = 0.606$). Per-type agreement ranges from $\kappa = 0.385$ (authorization scope) to $0.875$ (incompleteness) at $n \approx 17$ per type (Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_agreement}).

\paragraph{Human validation.} Two annotators, blind to LLM labels, labeled 100 stratified episodes. Inter-annotator $\kappa = 0.798$; human--LLM $\kappa = 0.733$ ($\kappa_w = 0.807$), approaching inter-annotator levels and validating the automated pipeline \citep{zheng_llm_judge_2024}. Per-type estimates ($n \approx 32$--$34$) have wide CIs (Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_agreement}).

\subsection{Controls}

\paragraph{Perturbation control.} To confirm specificity to semantic ambiguity, we run 300 episodes with non-ambiguity perturbations (synonym substitution, clause reordering). The perturbation effect is $\Delta_{\text{perturb}} = -0.67$ pp (95\% CI $[-8.0, 6.67]$, containing zero). Even under the worst-case CI bound (6.67 pp), the residual attributable to ambiguity is $\geq$27.3 pp. This control tests surface-level perturbation specificity; the information-addition confound specific to incompleteness is addressed separately via logistic regression (Appendix~\ref{app:confound}).

\paragraph{Within-family replicability.} A replication of 30 clause pairs $\times$ 2 models $\times$ 3 replicates yields ICC $= 0.919$ (95\% CI $[0.892, 0.940]$). ICC is computed within the OpenAI family; cross-provider concordance is moderate (Kendall's $W = 0.398$; \S\ref{sec:results}), and all seven models show $\Delta > 0$.

\subsection{Statistical Analysis}

We use McNemar's test for paired binary outcomes, $\chi^2$ tests with Cram\'{e}r's $V$ for omnibus effects, Fisher's exact test for per-type comparisons, and TOST equivalence testing \citep{lakens_2017} with a primary SESOI of $\pm 15$ pp. We report the matched-pair odds ratio from McNemar's test on discordant outcomes ($n_{\text{disc}} = 580$ of 1{,}497 pairs), which conditions on within-pair disagreement and thus differs from the crude marginal odds ratio. No industry-standard SESOI exists for LLM agent policy violations. We use $\pm 15$\,pp as a pragmatic threshold: it exceeds measurement noise (perturbation control upper CI: 6.67\,pp) by $>$2$\times$, and a per-type difference below this margin would not warrant distinct mitigation strategies. This is a pragmatic default, not a principled domain-anchored threshold; Table~\ref{tab:tost_sensitivity} reports sensitivity across seven margins ($\pm 5$--$20$\,pp; \citealp{anvari_lakens_2021}). The margin-free bootstrap cluster analysis (\S\ref{sec:results}; Appendix~\ref{app:cluster}) confirms the same equivalence structure. We apply Holm--Bonferroni correction within three pre-registered test families (Appendix~\ref{app:multiple_comparison}); post-hoc power exceeds 0.99 for C1 and C2.

