\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We organize results around three analyses: the binary ambiguity effect (C1), the type partial ordering (C2, including the inconclusive layer effect C3), and the model$\times$type interaction (C4). C1 and C2 are primary supported claims; C3 (layer effect) is inconclusive; C4 (model$\times$type interaction) is exploratory.

\subsection{Binary Ambiguity Effect (C1)}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_variance_decomposition.pdf}
\caption{Variance decomposition of violation rates. Clause identity explains 22.4\% of variance (of which only 0.4\% is between types), while condition (ambiguous vs.\ unambiguous) explains 15.8\%, model 1.0\%, and residual 60.8\%. Measured at temperature $= 0$.}
\label{fig:variance_decomposition}
\end{figure}

Across all five models and six types, ambiguous clauses increase violation rates by 34.0 pp (41.0\% vs.\ 6.9\%, conditional OR $= 15.1$, 95\% CI $[10.78, 21.17]$, $p < 10^{-105}$). All six types individually produce significant increases (all $p < 10^{-5}$), and all five models show large effects ($+25.7$ to $+39.0$ pp, all $p < 10^{-17}$). Perturbation controls confirm specificity to semantic ambiguity (\S\ref{sec:method}). Figure~\ref{fig:variance_decomposition} decomposes violation-rate variance\footnote{Type III SS; logistic regression (Appendix~\ref{app:regression}) confirms concordant results (Tjur's $R^2 = 0.175$; \citealp{tjur_2009}).}: clause identity explains 22.4\% (0.4\% between types, 22.0\% within), condition 15.8\%, model 1.0\%, and residual 60.8\% ($\sim$15\% stochastic, $\sim$46\% systematic; Appendix~\ref{app:residual}). A permutation test confirms that clause wording---not type membership---drives this variance ($p < 0.001$; Appendix~\ref{app:clause_variance}). The incompleteness effect ($+$49.2\,pp) is an upper bound that includes information addition. Incompleteness is fundamentally confounded: the unambiguous variant necessarily adds policy content (specifying what to do in the absent case), introducing an information-type confound that token-count matching cannot address. We therefore exclude incompleteness from our primary causal claim: the confound-free effect is $+$31.0\,pp (95\% CI $[27.9, 34.1]$). This confound may partially explain the $\tau^2$-bench vs.\ SOPBench effect gap ($+$34.0 vs.\ $+$12.7\,pp), as SOPBench uses naturally occurring policies where incompleteness confounds are diluted. The confound-free range is $+$18.7\,pp (coreferential) to $+$36.0\,pp (authorization scope).

Replication on two additional models confirms the effect across seven models ($\Delta = +20.0$ to $+33.6$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:model_expansion}); SOPBench~\citep{li_sopbench_2025} confirms directional consistency ($+12.7$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:sopbench}). The taxonomy predicts failure mechanisms substantially more strongly than violation rate (Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.45$ vs.\ $0.14$; the raw NMI ratio is inflated by target cardinality asymmetry; Appendix~\ref{app:type_mechanism}), making type-level analysis (C2--C4) informative for diagnosis.

\FloatBarrier
\subsection{Type Partial Ordering (C2)}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{Violation rates (\%) under ambiguous policy clauses by type and model (5.4 = GPT-5.4, 4.1 = GPT-4.1, Cla.\ = Claude, Qw3 = Qwen3, DS = DeepSeek). $\Delta$pp: pooled difference (ambiguous $-$ unambiguous) with Newcombe 95\% CIs. Tier: TOST equivalence cluster at $\pm 15$\,pp---U = upper bound (separated at all margins), M = mid-tier (6/6 pairwise equivalent), L = lower (separates at $\pm 12$\,pp). All per-type $p < 10^{-5}$ (Fisher's exact).}
\label{tab:main_results}
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.8pt}
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrrrr@{\hskip 6pt}cr}
\toprule
Layer & Type & Pooled & 5.4 & 4.1 & Cla. & Qw3 & DS & $\Delta$pp\,[95\% CI] & Tier & $p$ \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{\textcolor{speccolor}{Spec.}} & Incompleteness$^\dagger$ & 53.6 & 46 & 58 & 48 & 62 & 54 & +49.2\,[42.2, 55.6] & U & $<\!10^{-37}$ \\
 & Auth.\ Scope$^\ddagger$ & 41.6 & 64 & 46 & 12 & 26 & 60 & +36.0\,[29.1, 42.6] & M & $<\!10^{-22}$ \\
 & Cond.\ Prec. & 36.8 & 34 & 36 & 36 & 40 & 38 & +30.8\,[24.0, 37.4] & M & $<\!10^{-17}$ \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{\textcolor{lingcolor}{Ling.}} & Lexical & 42.8 & 42 & 34 & 32 & 52 & 54 & +35.2\,[28.0, 42.0] & M & $<\!10^{-20}$ \\
 & Scopal & 39.4 & 42 & 38 & 32 & 37 & 48 & +34.2\,[27.4, 40.7] & M & $<\!10^{-21}$ \\
 & Coreferential & 31.6 & 18 & 32 & 22 & 42 & 44 & +18.7\,[11.5, 25.7] & L & $<\!10^{-6}$ \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{2}{l}{\textbf{Overall}} & \textbf{41.0} & 41 & 41 & 30 & 43 & 50 & \textbf{+34.0}\,[31.3, 36.9] & --- & $<\!10^{-105}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\vspace{2pt}
{\footnotesize $\dagger$\,Subject to information-addition confound; see \S\ref{sec:results} and Appendix~\ref{app:confound}. $\ddagger$\,Lowest inter-judge agreement ($\kappa = 0.385$, per-type calibration $n \approx 17$); see \S\ref{sec:results} for construct subjectivity analysis. Excluding incompleteness: pooled $\Delta = +31.0$\,pp (95\% CI $[27.9, 34.1]$).}
\end{table*}

The six types are not interchangeable. An omnibus $\chi^2$ test on ambiguous-condition violation rates rejects the uniformity null ($\chi^2 = 28.02$, $df = 5$, $p = 3.6 \times 10^{-5}$, Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.137$). TOST equivalence testing across seven margins (Table~\ref{tab:tost_sensitivity}) reveals a margin-dependent structure: incompleteness separates at all margins; coreferential separates above $\pm 12$\,pp; the remaining four types form a stable mid-tier cluster at $\pm 10$\,pp and above. The $V$ comparison ($0.45$ vs.\ $0.14$) is threshold-independent. Table~\ref{tab:main_results} and Figure~\ref{fig:forest_plot} present the full breakdown.

Bootstrap clustering ($B = 10{,}000$; Appendix~\ref{app:cluster}) confirms stable extremes across all margins. Cross-model concordance is moderate ($W = 0.398$, $p = 0.072$; Appendix~\ref{app:kendall_w}).

An important qualification: three-family cross-judge validation yields concordant type rankings ($\rho = 0.71$--$0.89$; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_judge}), but two-group rankings are discordant ($\rho = 0.09$; Appendix~\ref{app:judge_group_ranking}), driven by authorization scope$^\ddagger$. Excluding it, $\rho = 0.90$ ($p = 0.037$); the remaining five types preserve the partial ordering. Authorization scope is diagnostically distinct, with lower agreement ($\kappa = 0.385$) reflecting construct subjectivity---whether \emph{investigating} an unauthorized request constitutes a violation.

Incompleteness ranks highest in four of five models ($\geq$46\% in all seven; Appendix~\ref{app:model_expansion}); its 49.2\,pp effect is an upper bound (Appendix~\ref{app:confound}). C3 (layer grouping) did not survive Holm--Bonferroni ($p = 0.017$, threshold $0.0125$).

\begin{figure}[!ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_forest_plot.pdf}
\caption{Per-type effect sizes ($\Delta$pp) with Newcombe 95\% CIs. Types form a partial ordering: incompleteness as upper bound (conditional on information-addition confound), coreferential as lower bound, and a mid-range equivalence cluster. Mid-tier ordering is margin-dependent (\S\ref{sec:results}).}
\label{fig:forest_plot}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Violation rates (\%) by model and type under ambiguous conditions ($n = 2{,}997$). Authorization scope varies 52\,pp across models ($\kappa = 0.385$), reflecting differential safety training; incompleteness remains uniformly high (46--62\%). C4 interaction is authorization-scope-specific (\S\ref{sec:c4}).}
\label{fig:heatmap}
\end{figure}

\label{sec:c4}C4 (model$\times$type interaction) is exploratory. The interaction is driven entirely by authorization scope (Claude 12\% vs.\ GPT-5.4/DeepSeek $>$60\%; Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap}); excluding it, the interaction vanishes ($p = 0.97$; Appendix~\ref{app:c4_sans_auth}). Cross-judge validation confirms model rankings (Fleiss' $\kappa = 0.717$; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_judge}).

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We organize results around three analyses: the binary ambiguity effect (C1), the type partial ordering (C2, including the inconclusive layer effect C3), and the model$\times$type interaction (C4). C1 and C2 are primary supported claims; C3 (layer effect) is inconclusive; C4 (model$\times$type interaction) is exploratory.

\subsection{Binary Ambiguity Effect (C1)}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_variance_decomposition.pdf}
\caption{Variance decomposition of violation rates (temperature $= 0$). Clause identity: 22.4\% (0.4\% between types); condition: 15.8\%; model: 1.0\%; residual: 60.8\%.}
\label{fig:variance_decomposition}
\end{figure}

Across all five models and six types, ambiguous clauses increase violation rates by 34.0 pp (41.0\% vs.\ 6.9\%, conditional OR $= 15.1$, 95\% CI $[10.78, 21.17]$, $p < 10^{-105}$). All six types individually produce significant increases (all $p < 10^{-5}$), and all five models show large effects ($+25.7$ to $+39.0$ pp, all $p < 10^{-17}$). Perturbation controls confirm specificity to semantic ambiguity (\S\ref{sec:method}). Figure~\ref{fig:variance_decomposition} decomposes violation-rate variance\footnote{Type III SS; logistic regression (Appendix~\ref{app:regression}) confirms concordant results (Tjur's $R^2 = 0.175$; \citealp{tjur_2009}).}: clause identity explains 22.4\% (0.4\% between types, 22.0\% within), condition 15.8\%, model 1.0\%, and residual 60.8\% ($\sim$15\% stochastic, $\sim$46\% systematic; Appendix~\ref{app:residual}). A permutation test confirms that clause wording---not type membership---drives this variance ($p < 0.001$; Appendix~\ref{app:clause_variance}). The incompleteness effect ($+$49.2\,pp) is an upper bound that includes information addition. Incompleteness is fundamentally confounded: the unambiguous variant necessarily adds policy content (specifying what to do in the absent case), introducing an information-type confound that token-count matching cannot address. We therefore exclude incompleteness from our primary causal claim: the confound-free effect is $+$31.0\,pp (95\% CI $[27.9, 34.1]$). This confound may partially explain the $\tau^2$-bench vs.\ SOPBench effect gap ($+$34.0 vs.\ $+$12.7\,pp), as SOPBench uses naturally occurring policies where incompleteness confounds are diluted. The confound-free range is $+$18.7\,pp (coreferential) to $+$36.0\,pp (authorization scope).

Replication on two additional models confirms the effect across seven models ($\Delta = +20.0$ to $+33.6$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:model_expansion}); SOPBench~\citep{li_sopbench_2025} confirms directional consistency ($+12.7$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:sopbench}). The taxonomy predicts failure mechanisms substantially more strongly than violation rate (Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.45$ vs.\ $0.14$; the raw NMI ratio is inflated by target cardinality asymmetry; Appendix~\ref{app:type_mechanism}), making type-level analysis (C2--C4) informative for diagnosis.

